WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good to have you back.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.940
This is Environmental Intelligence, episode 40.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.540
It's June 3, 2026.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.020
Here's what changed overnight

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.380
in the Environmental Regulatory Landscape.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.300
Alberta Oil Pipeline Root options

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.520
through Northern BC

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.500
trigger West Mobile League First Nations opposition

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.860
over territory impacts.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.400
West Mobile League First Nations Chief Roland Wilson

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.420
stated opposition to three mapped Alberta Oil Pipeline Roots

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.560
crossing Northern BC territory.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.020
Parallel coverage of Git Scan,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.460
Territory Agreements,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.700
highlights differing First Nations positions

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.580
on the same corridor proposals.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.060
Renewable Energy Developers,

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.700
Transalta and Boralex,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.160
continue advancing projects

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.140
under federal and provincial energy transition frameworks.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.100
Canadian practitioners should track pipeline consultation

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.940
and rooting requirements this week.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.200
If you only note one thing today

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.640
for your compliance calendar,

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.220
review current project files for overlap

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.920
with the three Northern BC corridor options

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.040
under ongoing First Nations consultation timelines.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.840
Pipeline proponents,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.100
contaminated highway sites assessors

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.600
and risk assessors working Northern BC

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.440
and Alberta border projects are directly affected.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.060
Alberta government documents obtained by CBC

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.580
mapped three potential oil pipeline routes

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.120
through Northern British Columbia.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.600
West Mobile League First Nations Chief Roland Wilson

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.600
stated his community opposes all three options

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.540
due to impacts on Territory.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.320
Previous routing processes in the region

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.060
have required federal and provincial consultation

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.280
under the Impact Assessment Act

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.820
and BC Environmental Assessment Frameworks.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.900
This means consultants supporting root selection

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.140
or baseline studies must incorporate updated

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.720
indigenous knowledge and rights based inputs

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.900
into environmental impact statements.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.280
Next steps include monitoring

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.640
any formal project description submissions

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.260
and associated section 35 consultation records.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.840
Alberta documents identify three Northern BC corridor options

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.460
for potential oil pipelines.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.660
Opposition from West Mobile League First Nations

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.020
triggers heightened consultation obligations

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.420
for any project advancing under federal

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.040
or provincial assessment regimes.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.640
No formal regulatory filing deadline

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.240
is stated in current coverage.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.700
Chiefs signed agreements stating

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.520
they were acting in the nation's interest.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.420
Others report limited choice in the process.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.660
This raises questions about consistency

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.780
of consent processes across affected BC nations

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.660
for the same infrastructure corridor.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.220
No qualifying peer reviewed

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.920
or government technical reports

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.540
with direct Canadian regulatory application

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.180
appeared in today's feed.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.080
Two Canadian renewable developers

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.720
continue advancing wind and solar assets

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.080
under existing provincial and federal permitting pathways.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.660
Practitioners supporting power sector clients

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.560
should note ongoing grid connection

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.700
and environmental assessment work streams.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.540
If you're working on pipeline

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.380
or linear infrastructure files in Northern BC

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.480
or the Alberta border region,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.540
review those files for overlap

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.020
with the three Northern BC corridor options

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.200
identified in Alberta documents.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.920
Update consultation tracking templates

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.560
to capture differing positions

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.340
among affected nations including West Mobile League

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:20.120
and Git San.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.180
Brief clients on the need for early integration

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.620
of indigenous knowledge into baseline programs

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:28.160
for any route advancing to formal assessment.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.480
Monitor federal and BC environmental assessment registries

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.040
for project description submissions

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.040
on these corridors.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.960
Alberta BC pipeline route consultation processes

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.780
continue with no fixed 30-day window stated.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.140
Renewable energy project permitting continues

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.860
under federal and provincial regimes

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.300
for trans-alta and borrelax assets.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.260
Monitor any new Canada Gazette notices

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.700
on pipeline-related regulatory amendments

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.940
or guidance updates.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.520
Now, speaking of pipeline corridor baseline data gaps,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.640
here's something I wish someone had told me

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.800
early in my career.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.140
You arrive at a scoping meeting

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.960
for a proposed linear project

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.240
crossing multiple First Nations territories

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.580
in Northern BC and the Alberta border region.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.540
The client has preliminary route options

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.380
but limited recent field data

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.880
on groundwater flow directions

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.400
and seasonal surface water connectivity

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.040
near proposed water course crossings.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.700
Under the Impact Assessment Act

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.440
and BC Environmental Assessment Requirements,

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.400
baseline programs must capture both Western science parameters

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.640
and indigenous knowledge on valued components.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.880
Experience practitioners notice that publicly available

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.360
sufficient geology mapping often under resolves

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.100
fine-scale hydraulic conductivity variations

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.420
that control contaminant migration

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:54.380
or spill trajectory modeling in low gradient boreal terrain.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.540
The insight that separates senior from junior work

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.780
is recognizing when desktop aquifer data must be supplemented

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.340
with targeted slug testing or packer testing

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.000
before route alternatives are narrowed.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.440
The most common mistake is treating all route options

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.260
as equivalent for hydrogeological risk screening.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.680
The fix is to run a rapid comparative vulnerability matrix

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.140
using existing well records and lidar derived drainage

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.340
before committing field budgets.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:28.620
Tomorrow, watch for any formal project description submissions

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.380
on the Northern BC corridors

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.500
that would trigger section 35 consultation records.

